Distance from the exact eigenspace to the computed discrete eigenfunctions on T_F.

Purpose
-------
On T_F the exact eigenvalue mu = (pi^2/4)*140 = 345.436... is DOUBLE, but the CG^2
Ritz upper bounds for lambda_2, lambda_3 have very different errors (27.06 vs 88.43
at refinement level 2).  This diagnostic tests whether that asymmetry comes from the
two discrete modes approximating the exact eigenspace E with different accuracy.

For each computed mode u_h^(k) we compute the distance to the exact eigenspace,

    sin(theta_k) = || u_h^(k) - P_E u_h^(k) || / || u_h^(k) ||

in both the L^2 and the H^1-seminorm, and the exact identity

    lambda_h - lambda = |u_h - u|_1^2 - lambda * ||u_h - u||_0^2,     u in E, ||u||_0 = 1,

which decomposes the eigenvalue error into eigenfunction-error terms.

Exact eigenfunctions
--------------------
Generalized sine functions of Jia-Li-Zhang 2021 (arXiv:2105.07547), Appendix
"Exact eigenvalues of homogeneous Dirichlet Laplacian on T_F":

    TS_k(s) = (1/24) sum_{sigma in S_4} (-1)^{|sigma|} exp(i*pi/2 * (k sigma) . s),
    k in Lambda_0 = { k in Z^4 : sum k_j = 0, k_0=k_1=k_2=k_3 (mod 4), k_0<k_1<k_2<k_3 },
    mu_k = (pi^2/4) |k|^2,     |k|^2 = sum_{j=0}^3 k_j^2,

with homogeneous coordinates s1=(-x1+x2+x3)/2, s2=(x1-x2+x3)/2, s3=(x1+x2-x3)/2,
s0 = -(s1+s2+s3).

|k|^2 = 140 is attained by exactly two indices, kA=(-9,-1,3,7) and kB=(-7,-3,1,9),
which are conjugate (kB = -rev(kA)), so TS_kB = conj(TS_kA) and the real eigenspace is

    E = span_R { Re TS_kA, Im TS_kA }      (dim 2).

|k|^2 = 80 (lambda_1) and 160 (lambda_4) are attained by self-conjugate indices only,
hence those eigenvalues are simple.

Independence
------------
This is a standalone P2-Lagrange re-implementation of the CG^2 space used by
Code/run_conforming_lg_rigorous.m (which uses the VFEM3D Bernstein basis).  Both span
the same conforming P2 space on the same mesh, so the Ritz values must agree -- that
agreement is asserted below as a validation gate.

NOT rigorous: this is a plain floating-point diagnostic, not an interval computation.
It says nothing about the certified bounds; it only explains the observed error split.


Lambda_0 index set, lowest |k|^2 (multiplicity = number of indices):
  |k|^2=  80  mu= 197.3920880218  mult=1  [(-6, -2, 2, 6)]
  |k|^2= 140  mu= 345.4361540381  mult=2  [(-9, -1, 3, 7), (-7, -3, 1, 9)]
  |k|^2= 160  mu= 394.7841760436  mult=1  [(-8, -4, 4, 8)]
